  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n\n- Beautiful, mild, and unseasonably warm weather continues through\n  Friday. \n\n- Gusty winds Friday afternoon will lead to near-critical fire\n  weather conditions across southwest Montana. \n\n- Confidence continues to grow for a sharp transition to a cold,\n  winter-like pattern this weekend. \n\nWarm and dry high pressure remains firmly in control across the \nNorthern Rockies for the remainder of this week. Beautiful and \nunseasonably warm days are expected through Friday, with high \ntemperatures running around 15 degrees above early October \nseasonal normals.\n\nOn Friday, ahead of the approaching system, gusty west to\nsouthwesterly  winds are expected to develop. The combination of \nthese gusty winds, warm temperatures, and dry conditions will \ncause near-critical fire weather conditions across southwest \nMontana during the Friday afternoon hours.\n\nConfidence continues to grow in a sharp pattern change arriving\nthis coming weekend. A strong cold front will traverse the region\nSaturday, dropping high temperatures significantly.\n\nPrecipitation and Snow Level Details:\nMoisture arrives Saturday afternoon and continues through Monday\nafternoon.  While there is still some uncertainty regarding exact\nsnow levels and the placement of the heaviest precipitation, \ncurrent long-term model runs are hinting that the bulk of the \nprecipitation will be focused across the Continental Divide. From \nthere, it is expected to spread westward across southwest Montana \ntoward the Idaho/Montana border, generally south of the I-90 \ncorridor.\n\nAs of today, models are showing snow levels by Sunday morning\nranging  significantly from north to south: dropping to around \n2,000 feet near Glacier National Park, around 4,000 feet near \nHomestake Pass, and hovering around 6,000 feet over Lemhi County. \nHigh-elevation passes and backcountry trails face the greatest \nlikelihood of winter travel impacts and slick conditions.\n\nFor lower-elevation valleys in western Montana (including\nMissoula,  Hamilton, and Kalispell), probabilities of receiving 1\ninch or more of snow remain relatively low (10-15%). However, as \ncold air deepens through Sunday and Monday morning, rain showers \ncould mix with or temporarily transition to light snow down to the\nvalley floors.\n\nBackcountry and Recreation Note:\nThose planning outdoor recreation, camping, or hunting trips in  \nhigh-elevation areas this weekend should prepare for full winter \nconditions, rapidly dropping temperatures, and treacherous travel \nover mountain passes.",
